{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "def monotonic(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return True is list elements are monotonically increasing or decreasing.\"\"\"\n\n    def _is_monotonically_increasing(l: list) -> bool:\n        # Checks if the list is non-decreasing.\n        if len(l) < 2:\n            return True\n        for i in range(1, len(l)):\n            if l[i] < l[i-1]:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    def _is_monotonically_decreasing(l: list) -> bool:\n        # Checks if the list is non-increasing.\n        if len(l) < 2:\n            return True\n        for i in range(1, len(l)):\n            if l[i] > l[i-1]:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    return _is_monotonically_increasing(l) or _is_monotonically_decreasing(l)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/59", "solution": "def largest_prime_factor(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return the largest prime factor of n. Assume n > 1 and is not a prime.\n    >>> largest_prime_factor(13195)\n    29\n    >>> largest_prime_factor(2048)\n    2\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_prime = 1\n\n    # Handle factor 2\n    # Divide by 2 until n is odd, updating largest_prime if 2 is a factor\n    d = 2\n    while n % d == 0:\n        largest_prime = d\n        n //= d\n\n    # Handle odd factors\n    # Iterate through odd numbers starting from 3 up to sqrt(n)\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        while n % d == 0:\n            largest_prime = d\n            n //= d\n        d += 2 # Move to the next odd number\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it means the remaining n is a prime number itself,\n    # and it must be the largest prime factor.\n    if n > 1:\n        largest_prime = n\n\n    return largest_prime"}
